  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provision and Upgrading of WASH Services by Rehabilitation of Water Supplies and Support of Improved Hygiene Practices</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Based on the needs assessments, access to safe and clean water is a challenge in many provinces in Afghanistan, where households rely on unprotected water sources, unstable piped water and, for a minority, surface water. Further, the lack of water points, and distance to collect water, also heightens the risk of gender-based-violence (GBV). Given the water scarcity and deteriorating hygiene conditions, there is also a high risk of public health threats, as seen with the recent acute watery diarrhea/cholera outbreak across five provinces. In addition, according to the 2020 Humanitarian Needs Overview, lack of access to water, as well as basic hygiene materials such as soap, is directly contributing to the spread of communicable diseases, leading to infections and deaths. This project is directly contributing to identified water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH) needs by expanding water provision through rehabilitation and protection of hand-dug wells and boreholes, repair and set-up of handpumps, and enhancing household water treatment and storage capacity. IOM is also providing hand hygiene infrastructure, hygiene kits and menstrual hygiene management kits and conducting hygiene promotion activities to improve public health and reduce the risk of WASH-related diseases in high needs and/or hard to reach locations.</narrative>
  </description>
